 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Causality:** The predictor must strictly use only previously processed voxels ($x-1, y, z$; $x, y-1, z$; $x, y, z-1$). If the decoder uses original values instead of reconstructed values, error accumulation will occur [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Non-stationarity:** The optimal predictor assumes wide-sense stationarity. Real volume data is not stationary, making global statistics suboptimal. The algorithm does not handle this locally (e.g., via octrees) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory vs. Speed:** The algorithm scans the volume four times (two for statistics, one for residual calculation, one for encoding) to avoid storing the large residual array. This increases computational time but reduces memory footprint [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Handling:** At boundaries ($x=0, y=0, z=0$), the neighbor terms are set to 0 (after mean subtraction). This ensures the prediction error is orthogonal to the input variables as per the derivation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Causality:** The predictor must strictly use only previously processed voxels ($x-1, y, z$), $(x, y-1, z)$, and $(x, y, z-1)$ to ensure the decoder can replicate the prediction exactly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Non-Stationarity:** The method assumes wide-sense stationarity (constant mean and covariance). Real volume data often violates this, making global coefficients suboptimal. The source suggests dividing the volume into an octree for local predictors as a future improvement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory vs. Speed:** The algorithm scans the volume four times (2 for statistics, 1 for residual calculation/frequency counting, 1 for encoding). Residuals are not stored to save memory, which forces the redundant fourth scan [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Handling:** At the boundaries ($x=0, y=0, z=0$), the corresponding neighbor term is set to 0 (after mean subtraction, this effectively means the neighbor contribution is 0). This ensures the prediction error is orthogonal to the input variables [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Compression Ratio:** Achieves approximately 50% compression (i.e., reduces file size to ~50% of original) on medical volume datasets [S1, unofficial]. For example, on `3dhead.vox`, it achieved 48.5% compression compared to 36.1% for gzip and 33.8% for zip [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On a HP 9000 Series 735, compression of `3dhead.vox` took 4.7 minutes, and decompression took 1.4 minutes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Entropy:** The method reduces entropy to near the theoretical limit. Test datasets showed entropies ranging from 3.28 bits/voxel (`sod.30.vox`) to 10.29 bits/voxel (`hipiph.vox`) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Significantly outperforms generic compressors by removing spatial redundancy. The optimal predictor automatically compensates for non-isotropic voxel sampling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High computational cost due to multiple volume scans and covariance matrix solving. Assumes stationarity, which may not hold for complex anatomical structures. The truncated Huffman code requires a fixed $L_1$ parameter, which is arbitrarily set to 256 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Compression Ratio:** Achieves approximately 50% compression (i.e., reduces file size by half) on medical volume datasets [S1, unofficial]. For example, on `3dhead.vox`, it achieved 48.5% compression compared to 36.1% for gzip and 33.8% for zip [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Entropy:** The method reduces voxel entropy to near the theoretical limit. Test datasets showed entropies ranging from 3.28 to 10.29 bits/voxel [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On a HP 9000 Series 735, compression of `3dhead.vox` took 4.7 minutes and decompression took 1.4 minutes [S1, unofficial]. The calculation of optimal coefficients takes only about 6% of the total compression time [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly outperforms generic compression algorithms (gzip, zip, compress, pack) by removing spatial redundancy. The optimal predictor automatically compensates for non-isotropic voxel sampling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Assumes global stationarity, which may not hold for all volumes. The four-pass nature makes it slower than generic compressors, though still acceptable for archival purposes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by James Edwin Fowler and Roni Yagel in January 1995 at the Proceedings of the 1994 Symposium on Volume Visualization [S1, unofficial]. It was designed specifically for lossless compression of biomedical volume data (MRI, CT, electron-density maps) where lossy compression is unacceptable [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm combines Differential Pulse-Code Modulation (DPCM) with Huffman coding, leveraging the spatial coherence of volume data [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by James Edwin Fowler and Roni Yagel in January 1995 at the Proceedings of the 1994 Symposium on Volume Visualization [S1, unofficial]. It is specifically designed for lossless compression of biomedical and scientific volume data where lossy compression is unacceptable [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm is implemented in the `compvox` tool [S1, unofficial].
 
